ndamentals": {"margin": 2.02, "lean": -0.75, "environment": 4.35, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -1.58}, "rating_margin": 0.62, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.45, "baseline_margin": -0.11, "analyst_adjustment": -0.39, "key_factors": ["October polls consistently show Jackson ahead by 1-3 points", "Prediction markets price Bottoms near 60% despite her polling deficit", "Jackson holds a massive spending advantage, raising $119M to Bottoms' $6M", "Open seat in a Democratic-favorable midterm environment; raters call it a Tossup"], "rationale": "Every October survey gives Jackson a narrow one-to-three-point lead, and he has vastly outspent Bottoms, but prediction markets and the Silver Bulletin tilt toward Bottoms on Democratic enthusiasm and the open-seat environment. The evidence nets out to a genuine tossup with a slight Republican edge on current polling.", "watch": "Whether Bottoms' late fundraising surge and outreach to Black and Latino voters close the polling gap, or Jackson's spending advantage locks in his narrow lead.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Brian Kemp (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Georgia's open governor's race between Democrat Keisha Lance Bottoms and Republican Rick Jackson sits at the center of the 2026 battlefield. Polls through late October show Jackson ahead by one to three points, with several outlets calling the race a dead heat. Seven of eight expert raters label it a Tossup, while the Silver Bulletin leans Democratic and all three major prediction markets give Bottoms roughly a 60 percent chance of winning.\n\nMoney is the sharpest asymmetry: Jackson has raised about $119 million to Bottoms' $6 million and has outspent her on the airwaves, though her late fundraising surge and a $1 million contribution from Gov. Pritzker have narrowed the gap. A Democratic-leaning midterm environment offsets the state's slight Republican tilt. Turnout among Black, Latino, and suburban voters will likely decide one of the country